  "body": "I propose to allow some semantically neutral elements that don't belong to Phrasing content category, at least the `div` element, to appear inside the `label` element.\r\n\r\nRationale:\r\n\r\n1. It works in all browsers.\r\n2. There is a widely used pattern of replacing the visually hidden checkbox/radio control with a custom one styled using the CSS selector like `:checked + .something`. Quite often these custom controls are built using `div` elements. These `div` elements must be the immediate siblings of the actual `input` element for this CSS to work, so if the input is wrapped with the `label` for usability and accessibility reasons, these `div` elements must also be inside it.\r\n3. There are some popular frameworks that implement and promote custom controls built this way, e.g. [Framework 7](http://framework7.io/docs/checkboxes-radios.html).\r\n4. There are cases in the modern Web apps where a label for a specific control needs more complicated structure that sometimes doesn't fit in the single paragraph.\r\n\r\nAlso, it seems that developers often ask for this (at least, questions like [this](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/18609554/is-div-inside-label-block-correct) and [this](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/29845376/is-using-a-html-label-to-wrap-elements-semantic) appear on StackOverflow quite often and seem quite popular).\r\n\r\nWouldn't it be useful to make the Content model of the `label` similar to the Content model of the `a` element (\"transparent\" with no Interactive content)?",
